---
// Interactive explainer of the brick-atlas PAGE TABLE used by the datasource volume renderer
// (playground/src/datasource/volumeRenderer.ts, ADR-0008). A 2D slice of the real 3D structures:
// a mixed-LOD selection → a low-res page table (one texel per finest cell) → a packed brick atlas,
// plus the streaming LOD-fallback walk-up that never shows a hole.
//
// Self-contained illustration (no import of the real renderer — it's a teaching diagram). Styles
// are `is:global` and namespaced under `.pt-doc` because the grid cells are created in the script
// at runtime, and Astro's scoped styles only reach elements present in the template.
---

<div class="pt-doc not-content">
<div class="page">

  <header class="head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Per-chunk LOD · single-pass GPU volume raymarch</p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <span class="chip"><b>Structure</b> &nbsp;3 textures, 1 pass</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>Source</b> &nbsp;volumeRenderer.ts</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>Decision</b> &nbsp;ADR-0008</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§0</span>Read&nbsp;me</h2>
      <p class="tight">A volume too large or too detailed to hold at full resolution is stored as a <b>pyramid</b> of levels, each cut into fixed-size <b>chunks</b> (here <span class="k plain">32³</span> voxels). A view wants different chunks at different levels — fine up close, coarse far away. The renderer must sample all of them in <b>one</b> raymarch. It does that with three cooperating textures. Everything below is a <b>2D slice</b> of what are really 3D structures, drawn small so the whole mapping fits on screen; the algorithm shown is exactly the one in the shader.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CONSOLE ============ -->
  <div class="console" id="pt-console">
    <div class="console-head">
      <span class="console-title"><b>Live map</b> &nbsp;selection → page table → atlas</span>
      <div class="legend" id="pt-legend"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-cap"><span class="idx">A</span><h3>Selection</h3></div>
        <p class="panel-sub">What the view <em>asked for</em> — a quadtree cut of the finest grid into chunks at mixed levels. Solid = brick resident; hatched = still loading.</p>
        <div class="grid" id="pt-selGrid"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-cap"><span class="idx">B</span><h3>Page table</h3></div>
        <p class="panel-sub">One RGBA8 texel per <em>finest</em> cell (<span class="k plain">pageDims³</span>). Each stores the atlas slot + level of the brick covering it. Tinted by the level it currently resolves to.</p>
        <div class="grid" id="pt-ptGrid"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-cap"><span class="idx">C</span><h3>Atlas</h3></div>
        <p class="panel-sub">Resident bricks packed into fixed slots of <span class="k plain">B³</span> voxels (<span class="k plain">slotsPerAxis³</span> of them). Filled as loads land; freed by LRU. Tinted by the level of the brick held.</p>
        <div class="grid atlas" id="pt-atlasGrid"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline">
      <button class="play" id="pt-playBtn" aria-label="Play streaming animation">▶&nbsp;&nbsp;Stream in</button>
      <input type="range" id="pt-scrub" min="0" max="26" value="26" step="1" aria-label="Streaming timeline" />
      <div class="tl-state" id="pt-tlState"></div>
      <div class="tl-legend"><span>◀ nothing resident</span><span>fully resident ▶</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="trace" id="pt-trace"></div>
  </div>
  <p class="console-hint">Hover any cell in A, B, or C to trace the mapping. Drag the timeline left to watch bricks stream in — coarse stand-ins sharpen as finer data lands.</p>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <!-- ============ PROSE ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§1</span>The three structures</h2>
      <div>
        <p>The <b>pyramid</b> (panel&nbsp;A) is the source data: level&nbsp;0 is full resolution, each coarser level halves each axis. A chunk at level <span class="k plain">L</span> covers <span class="k plain">2ᴸ</span> finest cells per axis — its <em>footprint</em> on the finest grid. A view's selection is a quadtree cut: a set of chunks at mixed levels that tiles the finest grid exactly once.</p>
        <p>The <b>atlas</b> (panel&nbsp;C) is one big 3D texture divided into a grid of equal slots. Every resident brick — <em>whatever its level</em> — is upscaled to a full <span class="k plain">B³</span> and copied into one slot with <span class="k">copyTextureToTexture</span>. Levels don't get their own textures; they all live together, packed. A free-list hands out slots; an LRU frees them under pressure.</p>
        <p>The <b>page table</b> (panel&nbsp;B) is the index that ties them together: a dense low-res texture with <em>one texel per finest cell</em>. Each texel answers a single question the ray needs at every step — <em>which atlas slot, at which level, covers this position?</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt">
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§2</span>The texel encoding</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Each page-table texel is four bytes — it fits the answer in an RGBA8:</p>
        <div class="encode">
          <div class="ch"><div class="cn">R</div><div class="cd">slot.x</div></div>
          <div class="ch"><div class="cn">G</div><div class="cd">slot.y</div></div>
          <div class="ch"><div class="cn">B</div><div class="cd">slot.z</div></div>
          <div class="ch"><div class="cn">A</div><div class="cd">level + 1<br />(0 = empty)</div></div>
        </div>
        <p class="tight mt-s">Storing <span class="k plain">level + 1</span> means a zeroed texel reads as <em>empty</em> for free — no sentinel needed. The level is what lets one slot serve cells of different sizes: from it the shader recovers how many finest cells share the brick. <em>(In these 2D panels the slot is drawn as (col, row); the real atlas is 3D and uses all of R, G, B.)</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt">
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§3</span>The lookup, per ray step</h2>
      <div>
        <p>The volume is marched <em>once</em>, in normalised array space where the box is axis-aligned. At each sample position <span class="k plain">p ∈ [0,1]³</span>:</p>
<pre><span class="cm">// 1. read the page table at p — one texel = one finest cell</span>
<span class="kw">let</span> pt    = <span class="fn">texture3D</span>(pageTex, p);
<span class="kw">let</span> mask  = pt.a * 255 &gt;= 1;          <span class="cm">// occupied?  (0 ⇒ empty)</span>
<span class="kw">let</span> level = pt.a * 255 - 1;           <span class="cm">// the resolved brick's level</span>
<span class="kw">let</span> slot  = pt.rgb * 255;             <span class="cm">// its atlas slot</span>

<span class="cm">// 2. recover the brick-local coord from the level</span>
<span class="hl">let cells = exp2(level);</span>                <span class="cm">// finest cells this brick spans</span>
<span class="kw">let</span> base  = <span class="fn">floor</span>(<span class="fn">floor</span>(p * pageDims) / cells) * cells;
<span class="kw">let</span> local = (p * pageDims - base) / cells;  <span class="cm">// 0..1 inside the brick</span>

<span class="cm">// 3. sample the atlas — the single texture read that returns density</span>
<span class="kw">let</span> density = <span class="fn">texture3D</span>(atlasTex, (slot + local) / slotsPerAxis).r * mask;</pre>
        <p>The elegance is in step&nbsp;2: <span class="k">cells = 2ᴸ</span>. A coarse brick covers many finest cells, so many adjacent texels point at the <em>same</em> slot; the level tells the shader to spread the brick's <span class="k plain">0..1</span> range across all of them. A fine brick has <span class="k plain">cells = 1</span> and maps one-to-one. Because the local coordinate is derived from the <em>stored</em> level, a coarse brick standing in for a fine cell is sampled <b>exactly</b> — which is precisely what makes the fallback below correct.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt">
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§4</span>Mixed LOD in one pass</h2>
      <p>Because every level lives in the same atlas and is addressed the same way, a selection that mixes levels (panel&nbsp;A: fine in one corner, coarser elsewhere) needs no per-level passes and no blending of overlapping bricks. <span class="k">rebuildPageTable()</span> walks the selected chunks and, for each, stamps its finest-cell footprint with one brick's (slot, level). The ray reads whichever texel it lands on and samples once. Cost is one texture-lookup pair per step, regardless of how many levels are on screen — no overdraw, and a trivially correct depth write (one fragment per pixel).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt">
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§5</span>The fallback walk-up</h2>
      <div>
        <p>Streaming is where a naïve page table fails: a not-yet-loaded chunk is a transparent hole. The fix is a walk up the pyramid. When filling a selected cell, <span class="k">residentOrAncestor()</span> takes the finest resident brick covering it — the chunk itself, or the coarsest resident ancestor:</p>
<pre><span class="kw">while</span> (level &lt; levelCount) {
  <span class="kw">if</span> (resident.has(<span class="fn">key</span>(level, x, y, z))) <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="hl">that brick</span>;
  level++;  x &gt;&gt;= 1;  y &gt;&gt;= 1;  z &gt;&gt;= 1;   <span class="cm">// climb one level</span>
}</pre>
        <p>Its (slot, level) go into the fine cell's texel — and because step&nbsp;2 derives the local coordinate from that level, the coarse ancestor is sampled at the right sub-region. The result is <b>blur that sharpens</b>: a region shows up coarse immediately and refines in place as finer bricks arrive, never blinking through a hole. <em>Drag the timeline left to see it — the top-left quadrant falls back to the coarse floor, then snaps to full detail as its level-0 bricks land.</em></p>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li><b>The coarse floor never leaves.</b> The coarsest full-chunk-grid level is always requested and never evicted, so the walk-up is guaranteed to find <em>something</em> — a hole is impossible once the floor is resident.</li>
          <li><b>Retain-on-refine, for free.</b> Eviction protects any brick the page table currently references — including an ancestor standing in for a refining region — so a coarse stand-in survives exactly until its finer replacement lands.</li>
          <li><b>The floor is never drawn directly.</b> The rebuild iterates only the <em>selected</em> chunks; the floor is reached solely through the walk-up, so it can't overwrite a finer resident chunk.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt" id="scaling">
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <h2><span class="num">§6</span>How it scales</h2>
      <div>
        <p>The design is <b>bounded by the view, not the volume</b>. Every structure here is sized to the working set the camera can see, so cost tracks the <em>visible frustum</em> — not the whole image, which may be far larger than memory. The demo runs a single fixed ceiling; below are the walls it meets at very large sizes and the planned way through each. Crucially, the fallback walk-up (§5) means overflowing any of them <b>degrades quality, never correctness</b>: the image gets coarser, never holed or wrong.</p>

        <div class="walls">
          <div class="wall">
            <div class="wall-h"><span class="wall-n">Atlas capacity</span><span class="wall-tag">GPU memory</span></div>
            <p class="wall-now"><b>Today —</b> a fixed <span class="k plain">12³ = 1728</span>-slot atlas (a 384³ R8 texture, ≈ 54 MB). A whole-volume view at full detail that needs more bricks simply thrashes the LRU; the LOD fallback keeps it correct, only blurrier.</p>
            <p class="wall-plan"><b>Plan —</b> hold the resident set to the frustum working set (via the resource ceiling below) rather than the whole volume, and grow to a larger / dynamically-sized atlas.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="wall">
            <div class="wall-h"><span class="wall-n">Unbounded selection</span><span class="wall-tag">upstream</span></div>
            <p class="wall-now"><b>Today —</b> <span class="k">select()</span> returns every in-frustum chunk at the desired level. A whole-view-at-finest demands one chunk per finest cell — tens of thousands at 2048³ and up — which then drives every downstream cost.</p>
            <p class="wall-plan"><b>Plan —</b> the <em>resource ceiling</em>: <span class="k">selectWithinBudget()</span> raises a global level floor and retries until the working set fits (degrade-to-fit, ADR-0008 §5), reporting “out of memory” only as an honest floor. The renderer doesn't drive this yet — it leans on the fallback to stay correct past capacity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="wall">
            <div class="wall-h"><span class="wall-n">Dense page table</span><span class="wall-tag">GPU memory · bandwidth</span></div>
            <p class="wall-now"><b>Today —</b> one texel per <em>finest</em> cell across the whole grid, re-uploaded whole on every change. Its size grows with the finest resolution: 2048³ ⁄ 32³ = 64³ ≈ 1 MB, but 8192³ → 256³ ≈ 67 MB, where the whole-texture re-upload starts to dominate.</p>
            <p class="wall-plan"><b>Plan —</b> a sparse / hierarchical (multi-resolution) page table that addresses only resident regions, so its size tracks the working set rather than the whole finest grid.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="wall">
            <div class="wall-h"><span class="wall-n">Full rebuild per commit</span><span class="wall-tag">CPU · per-frame</span></div>
            <p class="wall-now"><b>Today —</b> <span class="k">rebuildPageTable()</span> clears and re-walks the whole selection on every selection change <em>and</em> every brick commit. A fresh stream of N bricks is therefore ~<span class="k plain">O(N²)</span> — fine at demo scale, quadratic at large N.</p>
            <p class="wall-plan"><b>Plan —</b> coalesce commits per frame and update only the dirty cells. Paired with the sparse table above, that removes both the whole-texture re-upload and the per-commit full walk.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>None of these is a correctness limit. Because a missing fine brick resolves to its coarsest resident ancestor, the honest failure mode under pressure is a <em>coarser</em> image that sharpens as capacity frees — the same “blur that sharpens” the timeline above shows. Turning that graceful degradation from best-effort into <b>bounded</b> memory is exactly what the resource ceiling adds.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <span><span class="lab">source</span> &nbsp;playground/src/datasource/volumeRenderer.ts</span>
    <span><span class="lab">decision</span> &nbsp;ADR-0008 · view-driven multiscale datasource</span>
    <span><span class="lab">note</span> &nbsp;2D slice of 3D structures</span>
  </footer>

</div>
</div>

